#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:6
msgid "Hygiene In Rust Macros"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:8
msgid "Declarative macros in Rust are partially hygienic."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:10
msgid ""
"They are hygienic with respect to: local variables, parameters, loop labels, "
"and the special `$crate` variable."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:12
msgid ""
"They are **not** hygienic with respect to: items, types, methods, and traits."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:14
msgid "Rationale"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:16
msgid ""
"Frequently, rust macros are used as shorthand to refer to existing types and "
"traits, e.g. when defining `impl`s. In this situation, hygienic macros would "
"always need to accept all relevant items as arguments, imposing a floor "
"beneath which we could not decrease lexical boilerplate."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:21
msgid ""
"On the other hand, hygiene helps us write reliable code, so it is desirable "
"for any internal operations that a macro may want to perform. Luckily for "
"us, Rust does provide a solution for hygienic references to items."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:25
msgid "Example: Non-Hygienic Paths"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:28
msgid ""
"// Item reference is not hygienic! This macro may refer to a different "
"\"print\"\n"
"// function when invoked in different contexts.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:41
msgid "\"::a::print()\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:46
msgid "\"::print()\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:55
msgid ""
"The two expansions of the `call_print` macro refer to different items, "
"depending on the calling context. This could be useful as mere shorthand, "
"but is not robustly useful for referring to one particular item."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene/rust-macro-hygiene.md:62
msgid ""
"Explain that to enforce hygiene on local variables, the compiler keeps track "
"of \"syntax contexts.\" A local variable defined inside the macro has a "
"different syntax context than a variable of the same name defined outside, "
"which prevents collisions."
msgstr ""
